<div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
<h3 itemprop="name">Does the reMarkable support EPUB files?</h3>
<div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
<div itemprop="text">
<p>Yes. reMarkable tablets have supported reading EPUB files alongside PDF for several release cycles. You can sideload an .epub through the reMarkable desktop or mobile app, the email-to-tablet address, or drag-and-drop in the Web App. The software 3.28 update, released in early September 2026, overhauls the EPUB reading UI with better fonts, layout, formatting, and expanded language support.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
<h3 itemprop="name">What did software 3.28 change about EPUB reading on reMarkable?</h3>
<div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
<div itemprop="text">
<p>Per reMarkable's 3.28 release notes and coverage by Good e-Reader and Android Authority, the update redesigns the EPUB reading UI, improves text formatting and layout, adds new font options and more prominent font/layout controls, improves book cover thumbnails in the library, and expands supported languages. The changes are aimed at making reflowable EPUB reading feel closer in quality to reMarkable's historically strong PDF experience.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
<h3 itemprop="name">Do I still need to convert EPUB files to PDF for reMarkable?</h3>
<div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
<div itemprop="text">
<p>For text-based EPUBs (novels, nonfiction, reports), the 3.28 refresh makes reading the .epub directly a reasonable choice without conversion. But for complex files &mdash; fixed-layout children's books, heavily illustrated or design-heavy titles, or documents with tables that must not reflow &mdash; converting to PDF can still give better results because PDF preserves exact page layout. Whether reflow or fixed layout serves you better is the EPUB-vs-PDF question.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
<h3 itemprop="name">Which reMarkable devices receive the 3.28 update?</h3>
<div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
<div itemprop="text">
<p>reMarkable distributes its software update to the current paper-tablet lineup it actively supports, which includes the reMarkable Paper family and the older reMarkable 2. The color-specific improvements in 3.28 &mdash; such as the refined red-tone rendering for certain brushes &mdash; apply on reMarkable's color-capable Paper models, while the EPUB reading refresh, Capture Tool, new pen colors, and cloud import roll out across the supported line. Check the release notes on your tablet (or reMarkable's support site) to confirm the 3.28 update has reached your device.</p>
</div>
